<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>AI Summer Camp - Task 1</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/task1.css">
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@300;400;600;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.4.0/css/all.min.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="task-card">
            <div class="header-section">
                <a href="/lobby" class="back-btn">
                    <i class="fas fa-arrow-left"></i>
                    <%= user.role === 'INSTRUCTOR' ? 'Back to Leaderboard' : 'Back to Lobby' %>
                </a>
                <a href="/logout" class="logout-btn">
                    <i class="fas fa-sign-out-alt"></i>
                    Logout
                </a>
            </div>

            <div class="task-header">
                <h1 class="task-title">Python Programming Logic Challenge</h1>
                <p class="task-subtitle">Apply your understanding of Python concepts to solve these thoughtful problems.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- Quiz Section -->
            <div class="quiz-section">
                <h2 class="section-title">Think Through These 20 Challenges</h2>
                <p class="section-subtitle">Each question requires you to apply Python concepts we've learned. Think carefully about how each concept works.</p>
                
                <div class="questions-container">
                    <!-- Question 1 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 1: Code Analysis Challenge</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">What will be printed by this code?<br><code><span class="variable">numbers</span> <span class="operator">=</span> [<span class="number">10</span>, <span class="number">15</span>, <span class="number">20</span>, <span class="number">25</span>, <span class="number">30</span>]<br><span class="keyword">for</span> <span class="variable">i</span>, <span class="variable">num</span> <span class="keyword">in</span> <span class="function">enumerate</span>(<span class="variable">numbers</span>):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">if</span> <span class="variable">num</span> <span class="operator">%</span> <span class="number">15</span> <span class="operator">==</span> <span class="number">0</span>:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="function">print</span>(<span class="string">f"Position {<span class="variable">i</span>}: {<span class="variable">num</span>}"</span>)</code></p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q1" value="a"> a) Position 1: 15</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q1" value="b"> b) Position 1: 15, Position 3: 25</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q1" value="c"> c) Position 1: 15, Position 4: 30</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q1" value="d"> d) Nothing prints</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 2 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 2: List Operation Analysis</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">What will this code output?<br><code><span class="variable">scores</span> <span class="operator">=</span> [<span class="number">85</span>, <span class="number">92</span>, <span class="number">78</span>, <span class="number">96</span>, <span class="number">89</span>]<br><span class="variable">high_scores</span> <span class="operator">=</span> []<br><span class="keyword">for</span> <span class="variable">score</span> <span class="keyword">in</span> <span class="variable">scores</span>:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">if</span> <span class="variable">score</span> <span class="operator">></span> <span class="number">90</span>:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="variable">high_scores</span>.<span class="function">append</span>(<span class="variable">score</span>)<br><span class="function">print</span>(<span class="function">len</span>(<span class="variable">high_scores</span>))</code></p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q2" value="a"> a) 2</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q2" value="b"> b) 3</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q2" value="c"> c) 5</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q2" value="d"> d) 0</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 3 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 3: Data Structure Efficiency</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">True or False: When building a shopping cart system where items can be added, removed, and quantities updated frequently, a dictionary with item names as keys is more efficient than a list of tuples.</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q3" value="true"> a) True</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q3" value="false"> b) False</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 4 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 4: String Manipulation Strategy</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">You need to create a user greeting that handles missing information gracefully. Which approach is most robust?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q4" value="a"> a) f"Hello {user_name}!"</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q4" value="b"> b) f"Hello {user_name or 'Guest'}!"</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q4" value="c"> c) "Hello " + user_name + "!"</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q4" value="d"> d) print("Hello", user_name)</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 5 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 5: Loop Optimization Challenge</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">You want to find the first student with a grade above 90 in a list of 1000 students. Which approach is most efficient?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q5" value="a"> a) Check all students and collect those above 90, then take the first</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q5" value="b"> b) Use a for loop with break when first student above 90 is found</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q5" value="c"> c) Use a while loop that continues until grade > 90</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q5" value="d"> d) Use enumerate() to track position and continue checking all</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 6 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 6: Variable Scope Analysis</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">What happens when you try to use a variable that was created inside a function, outside of that function?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q6" value="a"> a) It works normally</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q6" value="b"> b) It returns None</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q6" value="c"> c) It raises a NameError</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q6" value="d"> d) It uses the global value instead</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 7 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 7: Advanced List Slicing</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">Given <code><span class="variable">scores</span> <span class="operator">=</span> [<span class="number">85</span>, <span class="number">92</span>, <span class="number">78</span>, <span class="number">96</span>, <span class="number">88</span>, <span class="number">91</span>, <span class="number">82</span>]</code>, what does <code><span class="variable">scores</span>[<span class="number">1</span>::<span class="number">2</span>]</code> return?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q7" value="a"> a) [92, 96, 91]</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q7" value="b"> b) [85, 78, 88, 82]</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q7" value="c"> c) [92, 78, 96, 88, 91, 82]</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q7" value="d"> d) [92, 96, 88, 82]</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 8 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 8: Dictionary Manipulation Logic</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">True or False: <code><span class="variable">student_grades</span>.<span class="function">get</span>(<span class="string">'Math'</span>, <span class="number">0</span>) <span class="operator">+</span> <span class="number">10</span></code> is safer than <code><span class="variable">student_grades</span>[<span class="string">'Math'</span>] <span class="operator">+</span> <span class="number">10</span></code> when you're not certain if 'Math' exists as a key.</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q8" value="true"> a) True</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q8" value="false"> b) False</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 9 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 9: Complex Conditional Logic</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">A delivery app gives free shipping if order > $50 OR customer is premium OR it's a holiday. How would you write this efficiently?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q9" value="a"> a) Multiple separate if statements checking each condition</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q9" value="b"> b) if order > 50 or is_premium or is_holiday:</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q9" value="c"> c) Nested if statements for each condition</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q9" value="d"> d) if (order > 50) and (is_premium) and (is_holiday):</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 10 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 10: Input Validation Strategy</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">You're asking users for their birth year. Which approach handles invalid input most gracefully?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q10" value="a"> a) year = int(input("Birth year: "))</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q10" value="b"> b) Use a while loop with validation to keep asking until valid</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q10" value="c"> c) year = input("Birth year: ") and check if it's numeric</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q10" value="d"> d) Assume the user will always enter correctly</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 11 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 11: Function Design Philosophy</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">When should a function return multiple values using a tuple versus creating separate functions?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q11" value="a"> a) Never return multiple values - always use separate functions</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q11" value="b"> b) When the values are closely related and often needed together</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q11" value="c"> c) Always return multiple values to be more efficient</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q11" value="d"> d) Only when you have exactly two values to return</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 12 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 12: Range Function Mastery</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">You need to create a countdown timer from 10 to 1. Which range() is correct?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q12" value="a"> a) range(10, 1)</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q12" value="b"> b) range(10, 0, -1)</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q12" value="c"> c) range(1, 11)[::-1]</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q12" value="d"> d) range(10, 1, -1)</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 13 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 13: List Comprehension Challenge</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">True or False: <code>[<span class="variable">x</span><span class="operator">**</span><span class="number">2</span> <span class="keyword">for</span> <span class="variable">x</span> <span class="keyword">in</span> <span class="function">range</span>(<span class="number">5</span>) <span class="keyword">if</span> <span class="variable">x</span> <span class="operator">%</span> <span class="number">2</span> <span class="operator">==</span> <span class="number">0</span>]</code> produces the same result as manually using a for loop with if condition and append().</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q13" value="true"> a) True</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q13" value="false"> b) False</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 14 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 14: Memory and Performance</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">For storing the same GPS coordinates (latitude, longitude) multiple times in your program, which is more memory efficient?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q14" value="a"> a) [lat, lng] (list)</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q14" value="b"> b) (lat, lng) (tuple)</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q14" value="c"> c) {"lat": lat, "lng": lng} (dictionary)</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q14" value="d"> d) They all use the same memory</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 15 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 15: Advanced String Processing</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">You're processing user emails and need to extract the domain (part after @). Which approach is most reliable?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q15" value="a"> a) email[email.find("@")+1:]</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q15" value="b"> b) email.split("@")[1] if "@" in email else None</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q15" value="c"> c) email.replace(email.split("@")[0] + "@", "")</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q15" value="d"> d) email[-10:] (assume domain is 10 chars)</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 16 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 16: Algorithm Thinking</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">You need to find the second-highest score in a list. Which approach is most elegant?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q16" value="a"> a) Sort the list and take index [-2]</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q16" value="b"> b) Remove max, then find max of remaining</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q16" value="c"> c) sorted(set(scores))[-2] to handle duplicates</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q16" value="d"> d) Use two variables to track highest and second-highest in one pass</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 17 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 17: Class Design Principles</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">True or False: When creating a BankAccount class, the balance should be directly accessible (<span class="variable">account</span>.<span class="variable">balance</span> <span class="operator">=</span> <span class="number">1000</span>) rather than using methods like <span class="variable">account</span>.<span class="function">deposit</span>(<span class="number">1000</span>).</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q17" value="true"> a) True</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q17" value="false"> b) False</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 18 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 18: Debugging Logic Challenge</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">What would this code output?<br><code><span class="variable">data</span> <span class="operator">=</span> {<span class="string">"a"</span>: <span class="number">1</span>, <span class="string">"b"</span>: <span class="number">2</span>, <span class="string">"c"</span>: <span class="number">3</span>}<br><span class="variable">result</span> <span class="operator">=</span> []<br><span class="keyword">for</span> <span class="variable">key</span>, <span class="variable">value</span> <span class="keyword">in</span> <span class="variable">data</span>.<span class="function">items</span>():<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="keyword">if</span> <span class="variable">value</span> <span class="operator">%</span> <span class="number">2</span> <span class="operator">!=</span> <span class="number">0</span>:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="variable">result</span>.<span class="function">append</span>(<span class="variable">key</span>.<span class="function">upper</span>())<br><span class="function">print</span>(<span class="string">""</span>.<span class="function">join</span>(<span class="variable">result</span>))</code></p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q18" value="a"> a) AC</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q18" value="b"> b) B</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q18" value="c"> c) ABC</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q18" value="d"> d) 13</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 19 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 19: Conditional Logic Challenge</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">You need to categorize students by grade. Which logic correctly assigns letter grades?<br><code><span class="keyword">if</span> <span class="variable">grade</span> <span class="operator">>=</span> <span class="number">90</span>:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="variable">letter</span> <span class="operator">=</span> <span class="string">"A"</span><br><span class="keyword">elif</span> <span class="variable">grade</span> <span class="operator">>=</span> <span class="number">80</span>:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="variable">letter</span> <span class="operator">=</span> <span class="string">"B"</span><br><span class="keyword">elif</span> <span class="variable">grade</span> <span class="operator">>=</span> <span class="number">70</span>:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="variable">letter</span> <span class="operator">=</span> <span class="string">"C"</span><br><span class="keyword">else</span>:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="variable">letter</span> <span class="operator">=</span> <span class="string">"F"</span></code><br>What letter grade will a score of 85 receive?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q19" value="a"> a) "A"</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q19" value="b"> b) "B"</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q19" value="c"> c) "C"</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q19" value="d"> d) "F"</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Question 20 -->
                    <div class="question-item">
                        <h3 class="question-title">Question 20: Advanced Data Flow</h3>
                        <p class="question-text">You're building a gradebook system. Which design best separates concerns and maintains clean code?</p>
                        <div class="options">
                            <label class="option-gold"><input type="radio" name="q20" value="a"> a) One large function that handles input, calculation, and output</label>
                            <label class="option-purple"><input type="radio" name="q20" value="b"> b) Separate functions: get_grades(), calculate_average(), display_results()</label>
                            <label class="option-orange"><input type="radio" name="q20" value="c"> c) A class with methods for each operation and data storage</label>
                            <label class="option-teal"><input type="radio" name="q20" value="d"> d) Both b and c are good approaches depending on complexity</label>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                
                <div class="submit-section">
                    <button id="checkButton" class="submit-btn">
                        <span><%= user.role === 'INSTRUCTOR' ? 'Show Solution' : 'Check My Answers' %></span>
                        <div class="btn-icon">
                            <i class="fas fa-<%= user.role === 'INSTRUCTOR' ? 'eye' : 'check' %>"></i>
                        </div>
                    </button>
                </div>
                <script> const userRole = "<%= user.role %>"; </script>

            </div>

            <!-- Feedback Section -->
            <div id="feedback" class="feedback-section hidden">
                <h3 class="feedback-title">Your Analysis Results</h3>
                <div id="score-display" class="score-display"></div>
                <ul id="feedback-list" class="feedback-list"></ul>
                
                <!-- Educational Explanation -->
                <div id="explanation" class="explanation-section">
                    <h4 class="explanation-title">Programming Mindset Developed!</h4>
                    <p class="explanation-text">
                        You've just practiced thinking like a programmer! Each question required you to analyze problems, 
                        consider trade-offs, and choose the most appropriate solution - exactly what professional developers 
                        do every day when building software systems.
                    </p>
                    <p class="explanation-text">
                        These decision-making skills - choosing the right data types, designing clear code structure, 
                        and thinking about maintainability - are what separate good programmers from great ones. In AI 
                        development, this same analytical thinking helps us design algorithms, process data efficiently, 
                        and build robust machine learning systems.
                    </p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="next-section">
                    <button id="nextButton" class="next-btn">
                        <span>Finish</span>
                        <div class="btn-icon">
                            <i class="fas fa-arrow-right"></i>
                        </div>
                    </button>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <script src="/js/task1.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
